{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in selected current DailyMed label.","fda_source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/009170Orig1s040lbl.pdf"],"proposed_value":"No FDA boxed warning identified in FDA-approved Mysoline (primidone) label (NDA 009170/S-040).","rationale":"The FDA/accessdata label contains WARNINGS but no BOXED WARNING section. The conclusion is supported, but DailyMed is not an acceptable source for this field.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"N/A 0%: FDA label lists ataxia, vertigo, nausea, anorexia, vomiting, fatigue, hyperirritability, emotional disturbances, sexual impotency, diplopia, nystagmus, drowsiness, skin eruptions, granulocytopenia, agranulocytosis, red-cell hypoplasia/aplasia, and megaloblastic anemia without quantified incidence percentages.","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"N/A: FDA label lists ataxia, vertigo, nausea, anorexia, vomiting, fatigue, hyperirritability, emotional disturbances, sexual impotency, diplopia, nystagmus, drowsiness, morbilliform skin eruptions, granulocytopenia, agranulocytosis, red-cell hypoplasia/aplasia, and megaloblastic anemia without quantified incidence percentages.","rationale":"The adverse reaction list is supported by the FDA label, but the '0%' wording is not supported and could be misread as an incidence value.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/009170Orig1s040lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"","rationale":"No alternate generic name was identified; phenobarbital and PEMA are metabolites, not alternate generic names for primidone.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/009170Orig1s040lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548512/"]},{"current_value":"Yes","field":"available_in_us","proposed_value":"Yes","rationale":"FDA's March 2026 Approved Drug Product List index includes MYSOLINE, PRIMIDONE and PRIMIDONE, PRIMIDONE; the FDA label identifies Mysoline tablets for U.S. prescription use.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/71494/download?attachment=","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/009170Orig1s040lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Oral tablet","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Oral tablet","rationale":"FDA labeling describes Mysoline 50 mg and 250 mg tablets.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/009170Orig1s040lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Focal; Generalized tonic-clonic","field":"epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Focal; Generalized tonic-clonic","rationale":"FDA labeling indicates Mysoline for grand mal/generalized tonic-clonic, psychomotor/complex partial, and focal/partial epileptic seizures.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/009170Orig1s040lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Mysoline; Primidone","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Mysoline; Myidone; Sertan; Apo-Primidone; Primidone (generic product name)","rationale":"Mysoline and Primidone/generic product names are supported by FDA sources. NCBI LiverTox also lists Myidone, Sertan, and Apo-Primidone as brand names, so the current alias list is incomplete.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/media/71494/download?attachment=","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548512/"]},{"current_value":"Mysoline","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"Mysoline; Myidone; Sertan; Apo-Primidone","rationale":"Mysoline is supported; additional brand aliases listed by NCBI should be included to avoid duplicate-name misses in literature searches.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548512/"]},{"current_value":"FDA labeling states the mechanism of primidone antiepileptic action is not known; primidone and its metabolites phenobarbital and phenylethylmalonamide have anticonvulsant activity.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"FDA labeling states the mechanism of primidone antiepileptic action is not known; primidone and its metabolites phenobarbital and phenylethylmalonamide have anticonvulsant activity.","rationale":"This matches FDA labeling. StatPearls discusses possible sodium-channel and GABA-A effects, but still describes the mechanism as not well-known, so limited confidence is appropriate.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/009170Orig1s040lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562297/"]},{"current_value":"Limited","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"Limited","rationale":"FDA labeling says the antiepileptic mechanism is not known; limited confidence is appropriate.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/009170Orig1s040lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"N/A - selected FDA/DailyMed label does not provide a half-life value.","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"10-15 hours for primidone; may increase in patients older than 75 years.","rationale":"The FDA label may omit half-life, but trusted NCBI StatPearls provides a primidone half-life of approximately 10-15 hours.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562297/"]},{"current_value":"N/A - selected FDA/DailyMed label does not identify a major metabolic organ; label identifies phenobarbital and PEMA metabolites.","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Liver; primidone is extensively metabolized by the liver to phenobarbital and PEMA, with substantial unchanged urinary excretion.","rationale":"NCBI LiverTox states primidone is extensively metabolized by the liver; StatPearls identifies phenobarbital and PEMA formation and unchanged urinary excretion.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548512/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562297/"]},{"current_value":"N/A - selected FDA label does not define clinically meaningful enzyme induction or inhibition.","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Enzyme inducer: NCBI LiverTox states primidone can induce CYP450 enzyme activities; StatPearls notes hepatic metabolism and phenobarbital-related induction of UGT/CYP2C/CYP3A pathways.","rationale":"The source-limited FDA-label statement misses a clinically meaningful enzyme-induction fact supported by NCBI sources.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548512/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562297/"]},{"current_value":"750 mg/day labeled day-10 maintenance target for patients 8 years and older; clinical effective serum level 5-12 mcg/mL.","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"750 mg/day labeled day-10 maintenance target for patients 8 years and older; clinical effective serum level 5-12 mcg/mL.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports Day 10 to maintenance at 250 mg three times daily and a clinically effective serum level of 5-12 mcg/mL.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/009170Orig1s040lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Patients 8 years and older: titrate over 10 days to 250 mg three times daily; usual maintenance 750-1000 mg/day divided; FDA label allows increase if required.","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Patients 8 years and older: titrate over 10 days to 250 mg three times daily; usual maintenance 750-1000 mg/day divided; FDA label allows increase if required.","rationale":"FDA labeling supports the day-by-day titration and usual maintenance of three to four 250 mg tablets in divided doses, with possible increase if required.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/009170Orig1s040lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"2000 mg/day; FDA label states daily doses should not exceed 500 mg four times daily.","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"2000 mg/day; FDA label states daily doses should not exceed 500 mg four times daily.","rationale":"FDA labeling states total daily dosage should not exceed 2 g and daily doses should not exceed 500 mg four times daily.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/009170Orig1s040lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No QT interval effect described in selected FDA label.","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"No QT/QTc effect described in FDA label; NCBI StatPearls notes a favorable QT-prolongation profile but uncertainty about QT-interval shortening.","rationale":"The FDA label does not describe a QT effect. A trusted NCBI source adds nuance but does not contradict the row's FDA-label-limited statement.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/009170Orig1s040lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562297/"]},{"current_value":"Approved Prior to Jan 1, 1982 (FDA Orange Book)","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"Approved Prior to Jan 1, 1982 (FDA Orange Book); NCBI StatPearls reports FDA endorsement for epilepsy on March 8, 1954.","rationale":"The Orange Book legacy wording is plausible and FDA's March 2026 index lists the product. NCBI provides a more specific historical FDA endorsement date that can be appended if the row allows precision beyond Orange Book wording.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-approvals-and-databases/orange-book-data-files","https://www.fda.gov/media/71494/download?attachment=","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562297/"]},{"current_value":"Unknown/limited","field":"filter_enzyme_effect","proposed_value":"Enzyme inducer","rationale":"NCBI sources support clinically meaningful enzyme induction, so the filter should not remain unknown/limited.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548512/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562297/"]},{"current_value":"Limited/unknown","field":"filter_metabolism","proposed_value":"Hepatic; renal excretion of unchanged drug","rationale":"NCBI sources identify liver metabolism and unchanged urinary excretion.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548512/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK562297/"]},{"current_value":"FDA Orange Book pre-1982 ASM; FDA-labeled for grand mal, psychomotor, and focal epileptic seizures.","field":"status_or_notes","proposed_value":"FDA Orange Book pre-1982 ASM; FDA-labeled for grand mal, psychomotor, and focal epileptic seizures.","rationale":"The label supports the seizure indications; FDA Orange Book sources support current listing and legacy approval wording.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2020/009170Orig1s040lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/media/71494/download?attachment="]}],"generic_name":"primidone","missing_rcts":[],"outcome_checks":[{"current_value":"N/A","field":"diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"N/A","rationale":